---
// My HCLSoftware case study, built from the Figma frames "MHS" (1680), "MHS / 1280",
// "MHS / 1024" and "MHS / Mobile 320". The blueprint for the other case studies.
// Rows sit side by side only in the 1680 layout (main 77rem and wider) and stack below it.
// Source order follows the stacked layout, except Project overview, where the paragraph
// introduces the list and must be read first.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Link from '../components/Link.astro';
import CaseFigure from '../components/case-study/CaseFigure.astro';
import Kpis from '../components/case-study/Kpis.astro';
import { url } from '../utils/url';
import hero from '../assets/my-hclsoftware/hero.png';
import journeyMap from '../assets/my-hclsoftware/customer-journey-map.png';
import downloads from '../assets/my-hclsoftware/marketplace-software-downloads.png';
import tablets from '../assets/my-hclsoftware/marketplace-tablets.png';
import indications from '../assets/my-hclsoftware/accessibility-indications.png';
import layouts from '../assets/my-hclsoftware/page-layouts.png';

const results = [
  { figure: '$2M+ saved', description: 'by building an internal platform instead of paying for an external one.' },
  { figure: '3 to 6+ out of 7', description: 'customer satisfaction, before and after release.' },
  { figure: 'Days to minutes', description: 'time to start a Marketplace trial.' },
  { figure: 'Tens of millions of dollars', description: 'in pipeline across the two portals.' },
  { figure: '40+ products', description: 'on My HCLSoftware.' },
  { figure: '59 products', description: '22 business solutions, 25 partner offerings on Marketplace.' },
];

const goals = [
  'An aligned experience across both portals',
  'Easier navigation',
  'International accessibility requirements met',
  'Lower costs for external portals',
  'Lower costs for design and development',
  'Standards that other external portals, such as documentation, could replicate',
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="My HCLSoftware case study"
  description="How My HCLSoftware was built from a blank screen in 2022, and how its design language became the foundation of Marketplace, BigFix and Mandala."
>
  <article class="case-study">
    <div class="hero">
      <h1 class="hero__title">My HCLSoftware case study</h1>
      <CaseFigure
        src={hero}
        alt="The My HCLSoftware Software downloads page on a desktop monitor, a laptop, a tablet and a phone."
        width={770}
        minHeight={465}
        eager
      />
    </div>

    <Kpis label="Results" items={results} />

    <div class="section text">
      <p>In 2022, before BigFix, before Mandala, Aaron Bauer and I opened a blank screen and began to build My HCLSoftware.</p>
      <p>
        This kind of project is every designer's dream and every designer's fear at the same time. The dream is simple: hardly anyone gets to build a product
        from nothing, exactly as they believe it should be. The fear is the other side of the coin, because when nothing exists yet, every decision is yours,
        and so is every mistake.
      </p>
      <p>
        We chose to treat the fear as the brief, and we began with the people rather than the pixels. Research came first, then the customer journey, so that
        every screen we drew answered something a real customer needed to do. On release, customer satisfaction roughly doubled.
      </p>
      <p>
        What we built did not stay in one portal. The design language and components became the foundation of Marketplace, then of BigFix, and now of Mandala.
        That is the kind of work I do: not standalone UIs, but foundations other teams can stand on.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="overview">
      <h2 id="overview">Project overview</h2>
      <div class="overview">
        <p class="overview__text text">
          The aim was to align two key customer-facing portals and create a set of designs and standards that would give them one consistent experience and
          serve as the basis for further portals. Marketplace is where customers discover, try and buy HCLSoftware products, and it is the strategic SaaS sales
          platform. My HCLSoftware is where they manage what they have bought: a self-service portal for accounts, relationships, assets and services. Six
          goals shaped the work:
        </p>
        <ul class="facts">
          {goals.map((goal) => <li>{goal}</li>)}
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="people">
      <h2 id="people">Starting with the people</h2>
      <div class="row row--text-first">
        <CaseFigure
          src={journeyMap}
          alt="The HCLSoftware customer journey map on a laptop, blurred, with its stages Marketing, Marketplace, My HCLSoftware, Support and Community."
          width={478}
          boxWidth={510}
        />
        <div class="row__text text">
          <p>
            The portals serve a wide range of users and flows, so the research had to be equally varied. It began with formative customer interviews to
            identify the personas and the pain points that already existed, continued with structured internal reviews that shaped an information architecture
            able to hold products with very different organisation and terminology, and included a beta, built both to evaluate the designs and to decide what
            to prioritise next.
          </p>
          <p>
            The customer journey map then gave the whole organisation one view of how a customer engages with HCLSoftware and which resources help them succeed
            at each stage. It informed everything from leadership conversations about direction to the UI decisions of individual project teams.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="origin">
      <h2 id="origin">The origin and the collaboration</h2>
      <div class="text">
        <p>
          Aaron Bauer is a principal designer I have partnered with closely for more than five years, through some of the most demanding projects of my career,
          and he now leads design across BigFix. I have learned more from him than from anyone else, about UX and about enterprise software, and our
          collaboration is one I hugely value. Ours has rarely been a divided practice: from MHS onward, we have built things with four hands rather than each
          retreating to a separate lane.
        </p>
        <p>
          A blank screen leaves no one to blame. There was no inherited pattern to explain an awkward choice and no legacy to excuse a compromise, so usability
          and accessibility were engineered in from day one rather than retrofitted later. Colour, breakpoints, the order in which focus moves through a page and
          the regions a screen reader announces were all decided together with the overall experience, never afterwards. It was the chance to show what we both
          believe: a design can be beautiful, usable and accessible all at once.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section marketplace" aria-labelledby="marketplace">
      <h2 id="marketplace">Marketplace</h2>
      <div class="pair">
        <CaseFigure
          class="pair__narrow"
          src={downloads}
          alt="The Software downloads page split down the middle, in light mode on the left and dark mode on the right."
          width={358}
        />
        <CaseFigure
          class="pair__bleed"
          src={tablets}
          alt="Three Marketplace pages on tablets: HCL Domino Marketplace, HCL AppScan Marketplace and Test Drive HCLSoftware."
          width={602}
        />
      </div>
      <div class="marketplace__intro text">
        <p>
          Aaron and I owned MHS. Marketplace was owned by Katie Rossi, a designer we worked with very closely and whom I still deeply admire and enjoy working
          with. Aligning our two products was a directive, but a directive says what to achieve, not how. Katie adopted the design language I had built, then its
          components and eventually its patterns, and every decision about how each one landed on her product was made with her, not for her. Some of those
          patterns we built together, and they belong to both of us.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="text">
        <p>
          Marketplace had a clear target: to increase online sales through SaaS and self-service. Starting a trial went from a wait of days or weeks to a matter
          of minutes, a result the whole Marketplace team earned and one the shared design language helped make possible.
        </p>
        <p>
          That collaboration held through a corporate rebrand a year later, when the system had to absorb new brand guidelines without losing what made it
          accessible or coherent. We integrated as much of the new branding as we could without compromising accessibility or usability, worked with marketing
          on the terms of adoption, and ended up sharpening the distinction between light and dark mode along the way.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="accessible">
      <h2 id="accessible">Accessible by design, documented and from day one</h2>
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="wrap__lead text">
          <p>
            I built the two libraries that hold all of this: the Accessible Design Language and the Accessible Components. Every component in them was built to
            WCAG 2.2 AA as a floor, not a target. Accessibility is the right and ethical thing to do, and it is also a legal and commercial requirement, because
            it allows the products to sell to every customer in the US and Europe, including government institutions.
          </p>
        </div>
        <CaseFigure
          class="wrap__figure"
          src={indications}
          alt="Three handoff sheets of accessibility indications, for page structure, heading structure and alt text."
          width={325}
          boxWidth={510}
        />
        <div class="wrap__rest text">
          <p>
            It is built into how designs are delivered as well. Every handoff carries full accessibility indications, from page regions and keyboard order to
            alt text, labels on icon buttons, heading hierarchy and component behaviour, together with the responsive design and spacing, so developers never had
            to make design decisions themselves or keep asking us for clarification, and QA always had something concrete to check against.
          </p>
          <p>
            Much of what we designed had to be templates for requirements that nobody yet knew, which is a very different discipline from designing a screen for
            a known need. It is part of why the foundation could later carry BigFix: the libraries proved stable enough to be used well beyond the product they
            were made for. MHS and Marketplace still run on them, and BigFix, a completely different set of products at a much larger scale, adopted them as its
            foundation too. They are now also the base of Mandala.
          </p>
          <div><Link href={url('/mandala/')}>See Mandala case study</Link></div>
          <p>
            I build things that scale, not single UIs, and this is the clearest example I have: one foundation that grew from a single portal to a whole product
            family without ever being replaced. That is the test I care about most: a system earns its name when a team building something different can start
            from it instead of starting again.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="responsive">
      <h2 id="responsive">Responsive and navigation</h2>
      <div class="row">
        <CaseFigure
          src={layouts}
          alt="Handoff sheets of page layouts at five breakpoints, from mobile to large desktop, showing the responsive behaviour and the spacing tokens."
          width={544}
          boxWidth={570}
        />
        <div class="row__text row__text--wide text">
          <p>
            A single set of breakpoints, from large desktop screens down to mobile, applies consistently across both portals, so the experience behaves the same
            on every device and meets accessibility requirements.
          </p>
          <p>
            HCLSoftware's customer-facing sites are built by independent groups and are, at best, loosely connected. We built a header system that lets users
            know at once that they are on an HCLSoftware page, makes it easy to move within and between portals, and is designed to support the portals that will
            join over the coming years.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section section--padded" aria-labelledby="outcome">
      <h2 id="outcome" class="narrow">MHS is where the standard started: beautiful, usable and accessible, together, never traded off against each other.</h2>
      <p class="narrow">
        I no longer design on it day to day, because BigFix and Mandala have my full attention, but I still give MHS UI feedback and advice regularly, and an MHS
        developer now takes part in Mandala's code reviews. Once Mandala is ready, MHS and Marketplace will adopt it, as BigFix will, so what began on a blank
        screen in 2022 is returning to the products it was first made for, now strong enough to carry BigFix alongside them.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section section--padded" aria-labelledby="next">
      <h2 id="next">Next case study</h2>
      <div><Link href={url('/bigfix/')}>BigFix</Link></div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .case-study {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space_32);
  }

  .hero,
  .section {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space_16);
  }

  /* The page title sits on the right from 760, as in the 1680, 1280 and 1024 frames */
  .hero__title {
    padding-block-start: var(--space_24);
  }

  @media (width >= 47.5em) {
    .hero__title {
      padding-inline-end: var(--space_16);
      text-align: end;
    }
  }

  .section--padded {
    padding-block: var(--space_16);
  }

  /* Paragraphs: 730 wide at most, 16 apart */
  .text {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space_16);
    max-inline-size: 45.625rem;
  }

  .narrow {
    max-inline-size: 45rem; /* 720 */
  }

  .overview {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space_16);
  }

  /* Stacked, the goals box comes first, as in the 1280, 1024 and 320 frames */
  .facts {
    order: -1;
    max-inline-size: 45.625rem;
    margin: 0;
    padding: var(--space_16) var(--space_16) var(--space_16) calc(var(--space_16) + 1.25rem);
    background: var(--contrast_background);
    border: var(--border_s) solid var(--tiles_border);
    border-radius: var(--radius_xl);
  }

  .row {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space_16);
  }

  .pair {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space_16);
  }

  .pair :global(.pair__narrow) {
    padding-inline: var(--space_8);
  }

  .pair :global(.pair__bleed) {
    padding-inline-end: 0;
  }

  .wrap {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space_16);
  }

  @container main (width >= 37.5rem) {
    .pair {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
      gap: var(--space_24);
    }
  }

  /* 1680 layout: text and figures side by side, Marketplace images below the first paragraph */
  @container main (width >= 77rem) {
    .marketplace h2 {
      order: -2;
    }

    .marketplace__intro {
      order: -1;
    }

    .overview,
    .row {
      flex-direction: row;
      align-items: flex-start;
      gap: var(--space_24);
    }

    .overview__text {
      flex: 0 1 45.625rem;
    }

    .facts {
      order: 0;
      flex: 1;
      max-inline-size: none;
    }

    .row :global(.case-figure) {
      flex: none;
    }

    .row--text-first .row__text {
      order: -1;
    }

    .row__text {
      flex: 0 1 45.625rem;
    }

    .row__text--wide {
      flex: 1;
      max-inline-size: 41.875rem; /* 670 */
    }

    .wrap {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 45.625rem) 510px;
      grid-template-rows: auto 1fr;
      column-gap: var(--space_24);
      align-items: start;
    }

    .wrap__lead {
      grid-area: 1 / 1;
    }

    .wrap__rest {
      grid-area: 2 / 1;
    }

    .wrap :global(.wrap__figure) {
      grid-area: 1 / 2 / 3 / 3;
    }
  }
</style>
